  $ karac build lbl_loop.kara -o lbl_loop && ./lbl_loop
  4000                                    <- correct
  $ valgrind --leak-check=summary ./lbl_loop
  definitely lost: 5,000 bytes in 1,000 blocks

The stack is `malloc <- karac_string_clone <- main`, so the clone is emitted at the BINDING SITE in the caller, not inside the accessor: codegen materializes the returned borrow pointer into an owned String value and never registers the result for drop. Scaling is exactly 1 block per invocation (1 call -> 1, 3 calls -> 3), chained or bound.

Contrast the `ref Vec[T]` accessor, which does not clone at all — it keeps the pointer — and leaks nothing over 300 chained calls under valgrind. So this is specific to how the String inner is materialized.

SEVERITY high because it is unbounded in a loop on an ordinary accessor shape, and there is no spelling that avoids it: binding first leaks the same.

Two directions, both unexplored: (a) do not clone — hand out the borrow the way the Vec leg does, which needs the consuming method to accept a borrowed String; (b) keep the clone but register the temp for scope-exit drop, which needs care not to free storage the receiver still owns.

ROOT CAUSE: the borrowed-receiver field-return DEFENSIVE COPY in `maybe_defensive_copy_param_arg` ran at the tail of a `-> ref T` accessor. That copy exists for the OWNED return (`fn name(ref self) -> String { self.n }`), where handing back the loaded `{ptr,len,cap}` would alias the receiver's buffer and both would free it. A `-> ref T` function returns no owned value at all: `compile_ref_return_ptr` supplies the address of the borrow's source and the tail value is discarded — `compile_function` already describes it as "a pure, dead load for the admitted shapes". It stopped being pure once the copy was injected.

WHY THE VEC LEG LOOKED CLEAN: it emitted the same dead clone. `karac_clone_Vec_i64` is pure LLVM IR over `malloc`, which LLVM DCEs; `karac_clone_String` tail-calls the OPAQUE runtime `karac_string_clone`, which it cannot. So the Vec leg was wasted work that happened to be free, and only the String leg surfaced under valgrind.

FIX: `maybe_defensive_copy_return_value`, a return-position wrapper that skips when `current_fn_returns_ref`, used at all three return sites (the `compile_function` tail, the mono tail, the explicit-`return` arm). The gate is deliberately NOT inside `maybe_defensive_copy_param_arg` — that helper also runs at ~25 ARGUMENT positions where a borrowed field read genuinely needs its copy (`v.push(self.name)` inside the same `-> ref String` function), and widening it there would double-free.

VERIFIED: the 1000-iteration loop, the chained and bound 3-call programs and the mixed-method program all go from N leaked blocks to zero under valgrind, with byte-identical output and interpreter parity. A program combining both positions — argument-position `v.push(self.name)` plus `stash(h, mut s)` across a `ref H` param, inside a `-> ref String` accessor — stays clean (0 errors, no leaks, `h.name` still readable afterwards).

TESTS: `ref_returning_field_accessor_emits_no_dead_clone` asserts the emitted IR directly — zero clone calls for the String AND Vec accessors (so the Vec leg cannot regress into a leak if its helper ever grows an opaque call), and a NONZERO count for the argument-position case so the gate cannot widen. Confirmed non-vacuous by disabling the gate and watching it fail.

BOTH BACKENDS AGREE, so this is the shared drop model, not a codegen-only defect.

MINIMAL REPRO (pure language, no networking):
  struct Res { id: i64 }
  impl Drop for Res { fn drop(mut ref self) { println(99); } }
  struct Holder { r: Res }
  fn main() {
      println(10);
      let r = Res { id: 1 };
      println(20);
      let h = Holder { r: r };   // MOVE
      println(30);
      println(h.r.id);
      println(40);
