  build, form A -> error: Module verification failed: "Terminator found in the middle of a basic block!"
  build, form B -> error: providers { } in { }: an early `return` ... is not supported

WHY. Both lowerings emit `karac_provider_push`, then the body inline, then `karac_provider_pop`. A `return` inside the body terminates the current basic block, so the pop would be appended after a terminator. Form A does exactly that and LLVM's verifier rejects the module; form B detects the terminator and refuses first, so the message names the cause instead of leaking an internal error.

NOTE THE BLOCK FORM'S REGRESSION. Before B-2026-07-31-9, form B COMPILED and printed a WRONG answer — the entire block lowered to constant 0, so `early2()` returned 0 silently. It is now a refusal. Loud beats silent, but a program that used to build no longer does, which is the practical reason to close this gap rather than leave it.

FIX SHAPE. Register the pop as a scope-cleanup action instead of emitting it inline, so it drains on EVERY exit path — the same channel user `Drop` and `defer` already use to reach the return path (`emit_scope_cleanup` / `emit_scope_cleanup_for_error_path`). That means a new `CleanupAction` variant plus its drain arms; `CleanupAction::UserDefer` has only ~5 sites (all in codegen/runtime.rs), so the surface is small. Registering it on the frame that the body's own `compile_block_with_frame` pushes — before the body's statements compile — makes the pop fire after body-local drops and before the return, which is the interpreter's order (`eval_providers_block` pops after `eval_block_inner` returns, including on `ControlFlow::Return`).

DO NOT 'fix' this by skipping the pop when a terminator is present. That yields valid IR and correct output for the simple case, but leaves a frame whose storage is an entry-block ALLOCA of the returning function on the provider stack — a dangling head. A later resource call reads freed stack, and a later pop hits the runtime's head==frame assertion. Silent corruption in exchange for a compile error is the wrong trade; that is why the block form refuses rather than skipping.

VALIDATION BAR: the same run-vs-build parity gate B-2026-07-31-9 installed, extended to an early-return body, for BOTH forms.

