SOURCE NOTE: found while baselining B-2026-07-30-11's displaced-value leg. Not caused by that work — measured on unmodified main (6057981), with the `let` form as a control.

MINIMAL REPRO (scalar field — the double body, no divergence):
  struct Res { id: i64 }
  impl Drop for Res { fn drop(mut ref self) { println(f"D{self.id}"); } }
  fn main() { let mut a = Res { id: 1 }; let b = Res { id: 2 }; a = b; println("x"); }
  interp / JIT / AOT all print:  D2 D2 x        expected:  D2 x
  (a's own displaced value never drops at all — that is B-2026-07-30-11, a separate leg.)

CONTROL, the clean bisect. The same move written as a `let` is correct on all three:
  fn main() { let b = Res { id: 2 }; let c = b; println("x"); }   ->  D2 x
So this is specific to the ASSIGN form of a whole-value struct move, not to moves in general.

SECOND, WORSE FACE — a heap field turns the double body into a run-vs-build divergence:
  struct Res { data: Vec[i64] }
  impl Drop for Res { fn drop(mut ref self) { println(f"D{self.data.len()}"); } }
  fn mk(n) -> Res { … push n elements … }
  fn main() { let mut a = mk(3); let b = mk(7); a = b; println("x"); }
  interp:  D7 D7 x
  AOT:     D0 D7 x        <-- the FIRST body reads a zeroed length
codegen's assign path calls `suppress_source_vec_cleanup_for_arg`, which routes a struct Identifier RHS through `zero_struct_move_caps` to stop the source's scope-exit StructDrop from double-freeing the buffers now owned by `a` (B-2026-07-16-18). That zeroing is right for MEMORY and invisible to a scalar field, but the source's user-drop BODY action was never retracted alongside it — so the body still fires, now on a moved-from value, and reads 0. The interpreter has no cap-zeroing, so it prints the pre-move length twice. Same defect, two different wrong answers.

WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE PRINT: a user `impl Drop` is how a Kara program releases a resource. Running the body twice is a double close / double release / double unlock, and the codegen face runs the second one against zeroed state. It is silent on both backends — no crash, no sanitizer hit (valgrind: 0 errors, 0 bytes in use at exit on both shapes), no diagnostic. That is the same silent-wrong-value profile the B-2026-07-30-11 entry flags as the reason its layer-3 work was unlandable.

LIKELY FIX SHAPE (unverified): the assign arm already decides `lhs_is_tracked_struct` and calls the memory-side source suppressor; the body-side sibling `suppress_user_drop_for_var(rhs_name)` is simply missing next to it, with the interpreter twin being a `moved_out_user_drop_bindings` record on the RHS identifier — the same pairing the ctor-arg move sites already do (`record_ctor_arg_moves` / the `try_compile_enum_variant` retraction). Whoever takes it should check the Map/Set and tensor arms of the same branch for the identical omission, and re-run the `let`-form control to confirm it stays single-fire.

RESOLUTION (session 01P2Hq5ojJ2qxkMoRq31zTQU, same fix commit as B-2026-07-31-39): the assign-move now retracts the source's UserDrop action (codegen suppress_user_drop_for_var at the tracked-struct assign block; interpreter suppress_assign_move_user_drop hook, sibling of the let-rebind hook) — one body, through the LHS, on both faces: the scalar double body and the heap-field zeroed-length first firing. Verified on both repro programs at three-way interp/JIT/AOT parity (D3 D7 x — the D3 is the displaced old value, the B-2026-07-30-11 leg this entry anticipated), valgrind 0 errors. Tests: e2e/test_struct_assign_move_heap_body_once.

SOURCE NOTE: found while baselining B-2026-07-30-11's displaced-value leg, on unmodified main (6057981), with a no-ctor-move control.

MINIMAL REPRO:
  struct Res { id: i64 }
  impl Drop for Res { fn drop(mut ref self) { println(f"D{self.id}"); } }
  enum Slot { Empty, Held(Res) }
  fn main() {
      let mut r = Res { id: 1 };
      let s = Slot.Held(r);      // r moves into the variant
      r = Res { id: 2 };         // r gets a fresh, independently-owned value
      println("x");
  }
  interp:      D1 D2 x      <-- correct: the payload's body, then r's NEW value
  JIT / AOT:   D1 x         <-- r's new value never drops

CONTROL — drop the ctor move and both backends agree:
  fn main() { let mut r = Res { id: 1 }; r = Res { id: 2 }; println("x"); }  ->  D2 x everywhere
So the retraction, not the reassignment, is what kills it.

Confirmed with a heap field too (`Vec[i64]`, lengths 3 and 7): interp `D3 D7 x` vs AOT `D3 x`.

MECHANISM. `Slot.Held(r)` moves the whole binding into the variant, so codegen retracts r's `CleanupAction::UserDrop` via `suppress_user_drop_for_var` — correct at that moment, since the enum's owner runs the body from then on. But retraction is a REMOVAL from the cleanup frame and nothing ever puts it back, so the fresh value `r` receives on the next line has no registered drop for the rest of the scope. The interpreter models the same suppression as a NAME in `moved_out_user_drop_bindings` and its `Assign` arm calls `rearm_container_bodies_for_name` after the store, which removes the name — so it recovers and codegen does not. The asymmetry is structural: a set entry can be un-set, a deleted frame entry cannot be un-deleted without re-registering.

IMPACT: a leak on the codegen side (the resource r's second value holds is never released) plus a run/build divergence, which by this repo's own bar is the worse half — a program that behaves differently under `karac run` and `karac build` with no diagnostic. Memory is otherwise clean (valgrind: 0 errors, 0 bytes in use at exit), so this is a missing drop BODY, not corruption.

LIKELY FIX SHAPE (unverified): re-register the binding's UserDrop action at the assign site when the store gives it a freshly-owned value — the codegen twin of the interpreter's re-arm. The let-site registration code in `stmts.rs` already knows how to choose the right fn (`track_user_drop_var` for a type with its own `Drop`, `track_user_drop_var_with_fn` + `emit_user_drop_field_bodies_fn` otherwise), so the work is calling that from the assign path rather than inventing anything. Interacts with B-2026-07-30-11's displaced-value leg, which touches the same assign site: whichever lands second should re-run both entries' repros. Check the `let`-rebind shape (`let mut r = …; let s = Slot.Held(r); r = mk();`) and the Vec/Map container-element retractions for the same never-re-armed pattern.

RESOLUTION (session 01P2Hq5ojJ2qxkMoRq31zTQU, same fix commit as B-2026-07-31-39): the identifier-reassign path now RE-ARMS the binding's UserDrop (track_user_drop_var) when the type declares impl Drop and no action is armed — the fresh value drops again (D1 D2 x at three-way parity). The displaced-value fire added by the B-2026-07-30-11 leg is gated on an ARMED action (new has_armed_user_drop) for exactly this shape's sake: ungated it replayed the moved-from slot's stale payload (D1 D1 x). Re-arm lands in the innermost live frame — a cross-scope reassign firing at the inner exit instead of the outer is the documented residual (was: never firing at all). Tests: e2e/test_ctor_moved_binding_reassign_rearms_drop.

SOURCE NOTE: found writing examples/sigv4.kara (AWS SigV4 request signing, the artifact examples/sha256.kara was built as a prerequisite for). The signer's header canonicalization sorts a `Vec[Header]` by moving elements into a fresh Vec; the interpreter produced signatures byte-identical to an independent Python stdlib reference on both test vectors, and the compiled binary aborted before printing anything.

MINIMAL REPRO (22 lines, no sort, no crypto):
  struct Header { name: String, value: String }

  fn move_all(headers: Vec[Header]) -> Vec[Header] {
      let mut out: Vec[Header] = Vec.new();
      for h in headers {
          out.push(h);
      }
      out
  }

  fn main() {
      let stamp = "20150830T123600Z";
      let mut hs: Vec[Header] = Vec.new();
      hs.push(Header { name: "X-Amz-Date", value: stamp.clone() });
      let moved = move_all(hs);
      for h in moved { println(h.value); }
  }

  interp : 20150830T123600Z          (correct)
  JIT    : free(): double free detected in tcache 2
  AOT    : free(): double free detected in tcache 2   (exit 134)
  valgrind: Invalid free() — the same 16-byte block freed twice, BOTH frees attributed to
            the `for h in headers` line of the consuming fn.

THE `.clone()` IS LOAD-BEARING, AND IT IS WHY THIS HID. Replace `stamp.clone()` with `stamp` and the program is clean on every backend. A string literal is a static slot with cap 0, so the second free is a no-op and the bug is invisible; the clone makes the field a real heap buffer and the second free is real. Any corpus whose struct fields are literal-initialized cannot surface this, which is a coverage lesson beyond this entry.

REDUCTION — what is and is not required (each measured against the abort):
  REQUIRED  a by-value `Vec[S]` parameter whose elements are MOVED OUT in a `for` loop
  REQUIRED  S carrying a heap field (String) whose buffer is genuinely owned (the clone)
  not req.  `insert(at, h)` — plain `out.push(h)` double-frees identically
  not req.  the sort, its comparison key, or any `.clone()` inside the loop
  not req.  a second element — one is enough
  not req.  a user `impl Drop` anywhere — this is plain heap-field cleanup

MECHANISM (read off the valgrind attribution, not the source): the destination Vec takes ownership of each moved element, and the source parameter's scope-exit cleanup still walks the same elements and frees their fields again. The move-suppression family that exists for whole bindings (`suppress_source_vec_cleanup_for_arg` → `zero_struct_move_caps`, B-2026-07-16-18) disarms a moved SOURCE BINDING, but nothing disarms the per-ELEMENT field cleanup of a container whose elements have been moved out one at a time.

RELATION TO THE OPEN DROP-GLUE WORK: adjacent to B-2026-08-01-22 / -23 but a different failure mode. Those are Drop BODIES not firing, in-parity, memory clean — leaks. This is a plain heap field with no user `impl Drop` involved, memory-UNSAFE, and out of parity. Whoever is in that code should check whether one disarm covers both.

SEVERITY: the shape is ordinary — consume a Vec of records, move each into a new one is what every sort, filter, partition and group-by looks like. It aborts rather than corrupting silently, which is the one mercy.

FIXED 7225d0a. Root cause confirmed exactly as the mechanism section guessed, one level deeper: the `for` loop binding is a shallow BIT-COPY of the container's element slot (`for_loop_owned_agg_vars`, the B-2026-07-04-17 model), and the push arm's move-suppression (`suppress_source_vec_cleanup_for_arg` -> `zero_struct_move_caps`) zeroes only the BINDING'S ALLOCA caps — which nothing drops — while the live owner, the source container's element slot, keeps its caps and is walked by the source's per-element scope-exit drain (`cleanup.adrop` loop). Destination + source then free the same field buffers. FIX (copy-depth == drop-depth, the same rule as the `let x = a` whole-move arm `deep_copy_for_loop_agg_element_move`): a new `deep_copy_pushed_for_loop_agg_element` hook in vec_method.rs runs right after the element store in ALL FIVE container-append arms (push/push_back, insert, try_push/try_push_back, push_front, try_push_front), deep-copying the just-stored element's heap in place at the destination slot — struct fields via `deep_copy_struct_heap_fields_in_place` (bare-`shared` fields rc-INC, same as the LET arm), value-ENUM live-variant payloads via `deep_copy_enum_heap_payload_in_place` (the entry-copy twin of the enum drop; the enum sibling `for it in src { out.push(it) }` over `Vec[Item.Named(String)]` was probed live with the IDENTICAL double-free and is fixed by the same hook). Gated to a bare Identifier arg in `for_loop_owned_agg_vars`, non-shared. NOT touched: the LET-move path (already deep-copied), enum LET-moves (probed clean pre-fix — a different channel covers them). Verified: the row's minimal repro + a local-vec sibling + a 6-element grow/insert/push_front mix + a Drop-carrying element (bodies fire once per owner on ALL THREE backends, identical order) — interp/JIT/AOT parity, valgrind 0 errors 0 leaks on every probe. Tests (all stash-proven failing pre-fix): e2e_for_loop_struct_elem_push_move + e2e_for_loop_enum_elem_push_move (tests/codegen.rs), interp