REPRO (the -26 repro with the closure renamed so the fixed name-shadowing lane is out of the picture):
  fn main() {
      let outer: Vec[String] = Vec.new();
      let mut grab = |x: String| {
          let mut v = outer;
          v.push(x);
          v.len()
      };
      let a = grab(String.from("a"));
      let b = grab(String.from("b"));
      println(a + b);
  }
  interp: 3 (a=1, b=2 — the first call's push is visible to the second)    JIT/AOT: 2 (a=1, b=1 — each call re-copies the empty env Vec).

Memory is CLEAN on the compiled side (valgrind 0) — this is a pure semantics divergence plus a checker hole, not a memory bug.

CONTRAST, both already consistent: the NON-move direct-mutation form (`outer.push(x); outer.len()` with `let mut outer`) prints 3 on ALL THREE backends (captured mutation is defined and write-back works); a single call of the moving form prints 1 on all three. Only [move-out of capture] x [second call] diverges.

DECISION NEEDED (design.md § closures / ownership tiers): EITHER (a) the ownership checker rejects a closure body that moves a captured binding when the closure can be called again — or, v1-simpler, rejects move-of-capture outright with a `.clone()` fix suggestion (the diagnostics-friendly option; Rust's equivalent is FnOnce-called-twice, an error) — or (b) the language defines the capture as re-read-per-call with alias semantics, in which case the interpreter's 3 is the oracle and codegen's per-call bit-copy must become a write-back (or the env must hold the value behind a pointer). Leg (a) needs a blast-radius check over the corpus (single-call move-of-capture is currently green and used).

