REPRO (probe b232/g1, six lines, aborts with exit 134; `karac run` prints the right answer): `struct E3 { msg: String }`, `fn g(i: i64) -> Result[i64, E3] { return Result.Err(E3 { msg: f"err-{i}" }); }`, then `match g(1i64) { Result.Ok(v) => {..} Result.Err(e) => { println(e.msg); } }`. AOT: `free(): double free detected in tcache 2`, SIGABRT. Valgrind: `Invalid free() / delete / delete[] / realloc()`. `karac check` is clean.  THIS IS AN ORDINARY SHAPE -- a struct error type behind `Result` is what most real error handling looks like -- and it is a CRASH, not a leak, which is why it is filed HIGH despite being narrow.  THE MATRIX, and every axis was measured rather than assumed:    CRASHES -- payload struct with a String (g1, 3 words), with a String built by mutation vs f-string (g1 vs g2, identical, so not a construction artifact), 4-word (f2) and 5-word (f4) payloads, scalar-first-then-String (h1), Vec-only (h2), and the Ok side rather than the Err side (g4).    CLEAN -- payload with NO heap field (h3); a BARE `String` payload rather than a struct (h4), which is the shape the machinery was written for; a WILDCARD arm that binds nothing (h5); the same struct through `Option` instead of `Result` (g3); a NAMED scrutinee instead of a fresh temp (g5); and a payload wide enough to be BOXED (f6, 4 words through `Option`'s 3-word area).  So: fresh-temp + `Result` + INLINE (non-boxed) + struct payload + at least one heap field + an arm that BINDS. Width is NOT the discriminator here, which makes it a different animal from the rest of this family -- 3, 4 and 5 words all abort.  MECHANISM, read from the code and consistent with all eleven probes: `track_freshtemp_inline_result_scrutinee` registers a `FreeInlineResultPayload` for a fresh-temp `Result` whose payload is inline heap. Its own doc says what it assumes -- "a `String`/`Vec`, or a transparent single-field wrapper" -- i.e. that the payload's `{ptr,len,cap}` overlays w0/w1/w2 of the Result aggregate. A STRUCT payload also puts a heap field's `{ptr,len,cap}` at those words when the field is first (and h1 shows a scalar-first struct crashes too, so the overlay assumption is wrong even about WHICH words), so the action frees that buffer. The arm binding then registers the payload struct's own drop and frees it again. The wildcard arm is clean precisely because no binding registers a second owner; `Option` is clean because this registration is `Result`-only; a named scrutinee is clean because it takes the ordinary binding path instead.  FIX DIRECTION, unverified: the registration should DECLINE a payload that is a user struct -- the arm binding owns it, exactly as it does for the named-scrutinee spelling -- or, if the no-bind case still needs covering, register a struct-aware walker instead of the `{ptr,len,cap}` overlay free. Check the wildcard arm (h5) after any change: it is clean today only because nothing frees, so a naive decline may turn this crash into a leak there. The boxed sibling (`track_freshtemp_boxed_enum_scrutinee`) already makes exactly this distinction via `inner_struct_name`, and is worth reading first.  PRE-EXISTING: reproduces identically with `src/` checked out at 165f306, this session's base commit, so it predates B-2026-08-04-5, -6, -9 and -10.  HOW IT SURFACED: probing the `?` ERROR-propagation side after B-2026-08-04-9 fixed the Ok side. The `?` spelling (b232/e3) aborts too, but that is downstream of this -- the plain `match` (b232/e5, g1) is the minimal form and `?` is not required.  ORACLE NOTE: loud on all three oracles -- SIGABRT under AOT, correct under `karac run` (a run-vs-build split), and `Invalid free()` under valgrind. Cheap to pin either way.  DIAGNOSIS CORRECTION (2026-08-04, same session, before any fix attempt -- the matrix above conflated two axes and the FIX DIRECTION it gives is wrong). Everything above is reproducible, but nearly every crashing probe used the same arm shape -- an f-string interpolating the payload's String field -- so "struct payload with a heap field" read as the trigger when it is only a precondition. The trigger is WHICH USE the arm makes of the bound field.  THE USE-FORM MATRIX (probes b232/k1..k5, identical `struct E3 { msg: String }` Err payload, fresh-temp `Result` scrutinee, only the arm body varies):    k1 `println(e.msg)` -- the field passed BY VALUE to a free fn -- CLEAN.    k2 `println(f"{e.msg}")` -- the same field INTERPOLATED -- DOUBLE FREE.    k3 `println(f"{e.msg.len()}")` -- a genuine read of the String field -- CLEAN.    k4 `println(f"{e.v.len()}")` -- the same genuine read, but the field is a `Vec` -- DOUBLE FREE.    k5 binds `e` and never uses it -- CLEAN.  So there are TWO sub-shapes, not one:    (a) f-string INTERPOLATION of a `String` field. k1 vs k2 is the whole story: the by-value free-fn arg is already handled -- `wrapper_arm_moves_heap_field_to_free_fn` exists for exactly it (B-2026-07-23-4) and suppresses the source drop -- and an interpolation is the same move wearing different syntax, which that predicate does not recognize. `consume_class` scores it as borrow-only, `borrow_only && wrapper` holds, the source payload drop stays armed, and it frees a buffer the interpolation already consumed. Probes f1-f5, g1, g2, g4, h1 are all this shape.    (b) `.len()` on a `Vec` field. k3 vs k4 is the whole story here: the same expression over a `String` field is clean and over a `Vec` field aborts, so this is not about moves at all -- it is the len-accessor intercept, the same family as B-2026-08-01-1 ("unwrap'd borrow-accessor row double-free at len intercept"). Probe h2 is this shape.  WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE FIX. The earlier FIX DIRECTION -- make `track_freshtemp_inline_result_scrutinee` decline struct payloads -- is WRONG and would be a real regression: k1, k3 and k5 all rely on the source drop firing, and declining would turn each of them into a leak. The registration is correct; the arm-side classification is what is incomplete. Start at the `if !(borrow_only && wrapper) || heap_field_moved_to_free_fn` gate in the match arm loop, and widen `wrapper_arm_moves_heap_field_to_free_fn` to count an f-string interpolation of a heap field as the move it is. Treat (b) as a separate leg and cross-check B-2026-08-01-1's fix before touching it.  STILL TRUE from the original write-up: the crash, the `karac run` divergence, `Invalid free()` under valgrind, the `Option`/named-scrutinee/ wildcard/boxed/no-heap immunities, and that it reproduces at 165f306. Width is genuinely not a discriminator. What changed is the ROOT CAUSE and the fix site.  LEG (a) FIXED (2026-08-04, 375dd77) -- f-string interpolation now counts as the move it is. `binding_fields_passed_to_free_fn_arg` also walks `InterpolatedStringLit` holes and notes a hole whose expression is a direct `binding.field`; a hole that is anything else (`{e.msg.len()}`, `{e.code}`, a call) reads rather than moves and is deliberately NOT noted, which is what keeps k3 and k5 clean -- suppressing there would remove the only owner and trade the crash for a leak. 24 of the 26 b232 probes are now clean, parity-EQUAL, vg=0. Suites: codegen 2779, memory_sanitizer 926, interpreter 1441, drop_differential 13, par_codegen 226 = 5385 passed, 0 failed. Pin: codegen.rs::e2e_fstring_interpolation_of_a_payload_heap_field_is_a_move, stash-proven RED, carrying the four-way use-form discriminator plus the three immunities.  LEG (b) STILL OPEN -- `println(f"{e.v.len()}")` on a `Vec` field (probes b232/k4, h2) still aborts. Unchanged by 375dd77 and correctly so: the `String` twin `f"{e.msg.len()}"` is clean, so it is not a move-classification question. It belongs with B-2026-08-01-1's len-accessor intercept; read that fix first.  A THIRD DEFECT the same sweep surfaced is filed separately as B-2026-08-04-12 (the `?` Err-staging path losing payload words past the third).  LEG (b) RE-CHARACTERIZED (2026-08-04, probes b232/m1..m7 and n1..n13; this SUPERSEDES the leg (b) description above, which was wrong on both of its claims). It is NOT about `.len()`, and it is NOT the len-accessor intercept (B-2026-08-01-1) -- disregard that pointer, it will waste a session.  WHAT IT ACTUALLY IS: a `Vec`-bearing struct payload of a fresh-temp inline `Result` double-frees as soon as an arm BINDS the payload, whatever the arm then does with it -- including nothing.  USE-FORM AXIS (all with `W1 { v: Vec[i64] }` as the Err payload): `f"{e.v.len()}"` (m1) ABORTS; `let n = e.v.len();` outside any f-string (m2) ABORTS; `e.v[0]` (m3) ABORTS; `e.v.is_empty()` (m7) ABORTS; and -- the decisive one -- an arm that binds `e` and NEVER MENTIONS IT (n6) ABORTS. So the use form is irrelevant; binding is the trigger. A WILDCARD arm (n7) is clean, which is the other half of that statement.  FIELD-TYPE AXIS, held constant otherwise: `W1 { v: Vec[i64] }` (n1) ABORTS and `W2 { msg: String }` (n2) is CLEAN; adding a leading scalar changes nothing -- `W3 { a: i64, v: Vec[i64] }` (n3) ABORTS, `W4 { a: i64, msg: String }` (n4) is clean. `W5 { v: Vec[String] }` (n5) is worse: SIGSEGV (139) rather than a glibc abort, i.e. a per-element walk over freed memory. Carrier and scrutinee shape behave as everywhere else in this family: the same `W1` through `Option` (m5) and behind a NAMED scrutinee (m6) are both clean.  OWNERSHIP IS PROVEN TO BE DOUBLE, and the source drop is one of the two owners: making the arm CONSUME the payload (`let w = e;`, which trips the existing suppression and disarms the source) makes the `Vec` case clean (n8) -- and the `String` case stays clean (n9), so the binding owns in both.  WHAT INSTRUMENTATION RULED OUT. Printing at both registration sites for n1 and n2 shows them IDENTICAL in shape: each registers one `FreeInlineResultPayload` with an overlay elem (`i64` for the Vec's element, `i8` for the String) and no struct-drop half, and each pushes one `StructDrop` (`__karac_drop_struct_W1` / `__karac_drop_struct_W2`). Two owners are registered in BOTH cases. So the asymmetry is not in WHAT