    }
  Replace the one `push_str(f"{i}")` with two literal `push_str("..")` calls (b232/r6) and it is vg=0. That single token is the whole difference.  THREE INGREDIENTS, all required -- each has an isolation control that keeps the other two and is CLEAN:    (1) the local's buffer grown by `push_str` with a NON-LITERAL argument -- r6 (literal args), r1 (one literal push), r3 (`let s = f".."`, no push_str) and r4 (built inline in the literal) are all clean;    (2) the local MOVED into a struct literal -- s1 (`push_str(f"..")` then `println(s)`, no struct at all) is clean, so the construction alone is not it;    (3) that struct reaching a match through a FRESH-TEMP `Result` with a BINDING arm -- s2 (same `mkx`, bound by a plain `let`, no `Result`) is clean, as are q1 (returned directly), q2 (`Option`), q3 (wildcard arm) and q4 (named scrutinee).  SO THE EARLIER READINGS WERE ALL SCAFFOLDING. "`.len()`" was wrong, "Vec field vs String field" was wrong (a `Vec` cannot be built without a local AND a runtime-sized `push`, which is ingredient (1) in disguise), and even "a local moved into the literal" was necessary-but-not-sufficient. Three corrections on one leg; the contrast pair r6/r8 is what finally pinned it, and any future characterization should be held to that standard -- ONE token apart, both directions measured.  WHAT THE IR RULES OUT, so the next pass does not re-walk it: `mkx` is CORRECT -- it emits `store i64 0` to the source `s`'s cap right after the move (`%move.cap.p`), so its own scope-exit free is properly disarmed. The two `main`s (r6 vs r8) are byte-identical after renaming, both emitting the `respl.suppress.at.w1..w5` payload-area zeroing AND a single `__karac_drop_struct_Sx`. The two `gx` bodies are identical. So NOTHING in the emitted structure differs -- the divergence is in the runtime VALUES, which points at what `push_str`'s non-literal path leaves in the `{ptr,len,cap}` versus the literal path, and at `karac_free_buf`'s size-hinted recycling (the two