SOURCE NOTE: found by a systematic tuple-vs-struct-field PARITY SWEEP (probe dir b240), not accidentally. Motivation for the sweep: 15 tuple rows were filed between 2026-08-01 and 2026-08-04, four of them high, and every one reads "the tuple spelling of a walk that handles the struct-field spelling" -- yet not one was found by looking. The sweep pairs each operation's struct-field spelling (the reference implementation, and a free oracle) against its tuple-element twin with identical semantics, and runs both on the interpreter and AOT under valgrind. 13 operations covered: destructure, method-on-element, index-of-element, compound assign, owned arg, ref arg, mut ref arg, scope drop, in Vec, in Option, element move-out, String element, nested projection.

SWEEP RESULT: 9 of 13 at parity, 3 divergences (this row is one), and one REVERSE-direction gap that is not a bug -- `h.inner.a[0]` loud-bails with "chained field receivers (`a.b.c`) are deferred to v1.x" while the tuple `t.0.0[0]` works, so the tuple spelling is AHEAD there.

NON-VACUITY: the field twin allocates 8 and frees 8; the counts below differ from that, which is what makes the divergence real rather than an optimizer artifact (B-2026-08-04-17). Seeded from `env.args().len()` with element reads throughout.

SHARED PRELUDE for every repro:
    fn mkv(k: i64) -> Vec[i64] { let mut v: Vec[i64] = Vec.new(); v.push(k); v.push(k + 1i64); return v; }
    struct H { a: Vec[i64], b: i64 }
  and `let n: i64 = env.args().len();` as the first line of main.

REPRO:
    fn bump(v: mut ref Vec[i64]) { v.push(42i64); }
  FIELD (correct):  let mut h: H = H { a: mkv(n), b: 5i64 };
                    bump(mut h.a);
                    println(f"{h.a.len()}:{h.a[2i64]}");  -> 3:42, 8 allocs / 8 frees
  TUPLE (BROKEN):   let mut t: (Vec[i64], i64) = (mkv(n), 5i64);
                    bump(mut t.0);
                    println(f"{t.0.len()}:{t.0[2i64]}");
                    -> panic at main: vec index out of bounds
                    valgrind: 8 allocs / 5 frees (the panic exits early, so the
                    free deficit is abort-time, not necessarily a steady-state leak)
  interpreter: 3:42, correct. AOT-only.

WHAT THE PANIC MEANS: `t.0.len()` is still 2 after a `push`, so the element never saw the mutation and `t.0[2]` is genuinely out of bounds. The runtime bounds check is doing its job -- the miscompile is upstream of it. Worth noting that the panic is what makes this VISIBLE; a `mut ref` mutation silently lost on a shape without a subsequent bounds-checked read would be a quiet wrong answer.

READING OF THE SYMPTOM, not a diagnosis: the callee most likely receives a pointer to a COPY of the element rather than to the element slot itself, so the push lands on a temporary that is then discarded. Check how the `mut`-marked call-site argument lowers a TupleIndex place versus a FieldAccess place -- design.md Feature 4 Part 1.5 governs the marker, and the field path demonstrably produces the slot pointer.

RELATED: B-2026-08-05-1 is the read-only `ref` sibling from the same sweep. Both are the tuple spelling of an argument path that handles fields; check for a single shared fix.  RESOLVED, and the two rows really were one bug -- the shared-fix guess on both rows held. CAUSE: B-2026-07-12-1 taught the ref-argument path to pass a POINTER to a struct FIELD instead of letting it reach the rvalue path, which shallow-copies the `{ptr,len,cap}` header into a temp and queues a scope-exit free of a buffer the owner still holds. That fix's own comment describes `scan(self.names, q)` aborting with `free(): double free detected` under AOT while the interpreter was correct. The TUPLE-element spelling never got the sibling arm, so it fell through and reproduced the identical abort one spelling over.  WHY ONE ARM FIXES BOTH SYMPTOMS: the `ref` case frees the temp (double free); the `mut ref` case MUTATES the temp, so `v.push(42)` grew a copy the tuple never saw -- `t.0.len()` stayed 2 and the following `t.0[2]` panicked. Same missing pointer, two failure modes. Worth noting which is the more dangerous: the panic is loud, but a lost `mut ref` write on a shape with no bounds-checked read after it is a SILENT wrong answer.  GATED exactly like the field arm -- only a `{ptr,len,cap}` element, so scalar and enum elements keep the existing path. Reuses `field_chain_place_ptr`, which already walks a tuple-index hop.  VERIFIED by re-running the full 13-case parity sweep that found these: `ref_arg` and `mutref_arg` now sit at 8 allocs / 8 frees, byte-identical to their struct-field twins. `in_option` (B-2026-08-05-3) is unchanged, confirming it is a separate site as that row predicted.

SOURCE NOTE: surfaced as a RED required CI job — `Codegen E2E (LLVM 18)` x86, `codegen_tests::test_e2e_option_result_map` — while the arm64 and macOS legs passed (the double-free is heap-layout-sensitive, so glibc tcache caught it on x86 where the arm64 allocator did not abort). This is the builtin-passthrough sibling of the B-2026-08-06-27/28/29 user-function passthrough series, and reuses that series' machinery.

ROOT CAUSE. The hand-rolled scalar-inner `Option`/`Result` `.map(f)` lowering (calls.rs, the `is_trivially_copyable_te(&inner_te)` path) compiles the ABSENT branch as `absent_result = recv_struct.into()` — a SHALLOW copy of the receiver's `{tag, w0, w1, w2, ...}` words. When the receiver is an `Err`/`None` whose payload is a heap `{ptr,len,cap}` (`Result[i64, String]` in `Err`), the map result ALIASES the receiver's buffer: both the result and the still-armed source binding believe they own that one boom pointer. The Ok/Some inner payload here is a scalar (a heap inner delegates to `compile_map_via_match_synthesis`, which owns the move-out), so the only heap is the source's Err/None payload.

WHY IT WAS SHAPE-DEPENDENT. `let r = <binding>.map(f)` alone was clean: the let-site tracking gate (stmts.rs) admits only `Call`/fresh-inline RHS, so a `.map` MethodCall RHS registered NEITHER a fresh owner NOR a passthrough alias — `r` was simply untracked and the source stayed sole owner (one free, by luck). The two CONSUMING shapes broke it:
  (b) `er.map(f).unwrap_or(d)` — `unwrap_or`'s absent path frees the receiver's Err payload (B-2026-08-05-9), and `suppress_inline_option_result_binding_move(object)` could not peel the `.map` MethodCall receiver to disarm the source `er`, so `er`'s scope-exit `FreeInlineResultPayload` freed the same buffer again;
  (c) `er.map(f);` in statement position — the discarded-temp registrar (`try_track_discarded_inline_result`) materialized `__owned_res_tmp` and queued a free, while `er` stayed armed;
  (a) `let r = er.map(f); r.unwrap_or(d)` — no let-site alias link, so the `unwrap_or` consume resolved `r` to itself (untracked, no-op) and `er` double-freed.

FIX. A new `map_passthrough_armed_source` detector recognizes `<armed inline Option/Result binding>.map(f)` and returns the source name, wired to the SAME three points the user-function passthrough series uses, in the SAME directions (measured, not assumed by symmetry — only `map` is recognized, other Err/None-passthrough combinators are left for follow-up):
  • discard (c): `discarded_temp_aliases_armed_source` NARROWS — the temp registers nothing, the source stays sole owner (never zero the source on a discard — B-2026-08-06-21's leak lesson);
  • consume (b): `suppress_inline_option_result_binding_move` peels the `.map` receiver and ZEROES the source slot, because `unwrap_or` already freed the aliased payload;
  • let-bind (a): the stmts.rs let-site records `r` as a `passthrough_owner_alias` of the source, so a later `r.unwrap_or(d)` resolves through it and zeroes the true owner.

Verified interp == JIT == AOT-O0 == AOT-O2 on the flagship program and each reduced shape; full codegen suite green on BOTH the AOT and JIT-parity legs; memory_sanitizer (ASAN+LSan) clean — no leak from the narrow/zero split.

MINIMAL REPRO -- 10 lines, no loops, no conditionals. Aborts under `karac run` (JIT) and `karac build` (AOT); prints correctly under `--interp`; `karac check` reports no errors:

    struct R { region: String }
    fn main() {
        let mut s = String.new();
        s.push_str("north");
        let mut stats = Vec.new();
        stats.push(R { region: s });
        let mut out = String.new();
        out = stats[0].region;        // <- ASSIGNMENT read-back from a Vec element
        println(f"{out}");
    }

    interp   north
    JIT      free(): double free detected in tcache 2
    AOT      free(): double free detected in tcache 2   (exit 134)

NECESSARY CONDITIONS -- each has a measured clean control:

    variant                                                          verdict
    struct is a Vec ELEMENT, read back by ASSIGNMENT                 ABORT
    ... field type is `Vec[i64]` instead of `String`                 ABORT
    ... with an unrelated earlier read of the same field             ABORT
    ... source built by `+` concat instead of push_str               ABORT
    read back by `let out = stats[0].region` (LET, not assignment)   clean  <- that leg is fixed
    struct in a plain BINDING: `let h = R{..}; out = h.region`       clean
    struct in a TUPLE: `let t = (R{..}, 1); out = t.0.region`        clean
    no struct at all: `let mut v: Vec[String]; out = v[0]`           clean

So: (1) the struct must be reached through a Vec INDEX -- a binding or a tuple element is fine; (2) the read-back must be an ASSIGNMENT to an existing binding -- a fresh `let` is fine; (3) the field must be heap-owning (String and `Vec[i64]` both reproduce). Loops, `if`, an intermediate `let r = regions[i]`, and the assignment target's seed value are all irrelevant -- present in the original find, absent from the minimal.

Not optimization- or auto-par-dependent: `KARAC_OPT_LEVEL=0` and `KARAC_AUTO_PAR=0` both abort identically.

MEASUREMENT CORRECTION worth recording, because it nearly went into this row as a false condition. Two early controls appeared clean and were NOT: `R { region: "north" }` (a String LITERAL field) and the same through a plain binding both ran fine, which suggested the rule needed the field to be sourced from another Vec's element. It does not -- a string literal is static, so the second free lands on a non-heap pointer and is invisible. Re-running with a genuinely heap-allocated source (`String.new()` + `push_str`, or `a + b`) reproduces with NO Vec source anywhere. Anyone writing fixtures for this must build the payload at run time; a literal payload silently passes. This is the same authoring hazard B-2026-08-04-17 documents.

WHY IT IS HIGH. The shape is routine reporting code -- build a summary struct per group, push it into a Vec, then scan for the best one:

    let mut best = 0;
    let mut best_region = String.new();
    for i in 0..stats.len() {
        if stats[i].revenue > best {
            best = stats[i].revenue;
            best_region = stats[i].region;   // <- the abort
        }
    }

That is the entire find. A double free is heap corruption, not a leak: `karac check` is silent, the interpreter gives the right answer, and the compiled binary aborts before flushing stdout, so the program appears to produce NO output at all rather than a partial one -- which reads like a startup failure and sends debugging in the wrong direction.

SUGGESTED FIXTURES: the eight-row table above -- four aborting pins and four over-fire controls. The controls matter here because three of the four are shapes an over-broad fix would regress (the `let` form, the plain binding, the tuple element), and all four must keep working.

