The original finding, from kata #266's toggle solver, is the natural spelling of a parity set over a string:

    for b in s.bytes() { if odd.contains(b) { odd.remove(b); } else { odd.insert(b); } }

Compiled, `contains` never reports true, so the remove branch is dead and `insert` is a no-op on an existing key: the set silently becomes the DISTINCT-characters set rather than the odd-parity set. "aab" -> false instead of true, "aabb" -> false instead of true. Note how well it hides: "aa", "zzzzzzzz", "code" and "aaabbb" all still give the RIGHT answer under the wrong set, so a small example set can pass entirely.

SCOPE (probed, not assumed). Broken: `Set[i64].contains(u8)`. Correct in the same program: `Set[i64].contains(i32)`, `Set[i64].insert(u8)`, `Set[i64].remove(u8)`, `Vec[i64].contains(u8)`, `Map[i64,i64].contains_key(u8)`. So it is `Set.contains` specifically, and only for the u8 width in the cases probed — though the i32 case is very likely working BY LUCK rather than by construction (see mechanism), so it should not be treated as a boundary.

MECHANISM. The `contains` arm allocas an `elem_ty`-sized slot and stores the compiled argument into it with no conversion. An i8 value stored into an i64 slot writes one byte and leaves seven UNDEFINED, which the erased `karac_map_contains` then hashes. The scalar-key mono fast path would have side-stepped this, but it is gated on `elem_val.get_type() == elem_ty`, so precisely the mismatched-width case falls through to the erased path that is sensitive to the undefined bytes. i32 presumably survives only because the surrounding four bytes of that fresh entry alloca happen to be zero.

DESIGN CALL FOR THE FIXER — there are two defensible fixes and this row deliberately does not pick one. (a) REJECT AT TYPECHECK. Kāra already refuses `b - 97i64` for a `u8` b with "cannot mix integer types 'u8' and 'i64' in arithmetic - cast explicitly with `as`". Container element arguments accepting silently is inconsistent with that rule, and rejecting would turn this miscompile into a compile error at the call site. (b) COERCE IN CODEGEN. Convert `elem_val` to `elem_ty` before the store (and before the mono gate), which also lets the mono fast path fire. If (a) is chosen, the `insert`/`remove` arms should be audited for the same silent acceptance even though they currently produce right answers - they are right by accident, on the same undefined-byte mechanism.

KATA STATUS. kata #266 spells the toggle as `let ch = b as i64;` and comments why. That is NOT a workaround for this bug: the sibling bitmask solver in the same kata was FORCED by the typechecker to write `b as i64` for exactly this width mismatch, so the cast is what the language's own stated rule asks for at both call sites. The inconsistency between the two call sites is the bug being filed.

