REPRO (auto-par; `karac build`, run under ASAN+LSan):
    struct W { f0: i64, f1: i64, f2: i64, f3: i64 }
    fn get(v: ref Vec[W], k: i64) -> Option[W] { ... return Some(v[i]); ... }
    fn tail(o: Option[W]) -> i64 { match o { Some(w) => w.f3, None => -1i64 } }
    fn build(seed: i64) -> i64 {
        let mut ns: Vec[W] = Vec.new();
        ns.push(W { f0: seed, f1: seed+1, f2: seed+2, f3: seed+3 });
        let hit = get(ns, seed);          // two independent lets -> forks
        let miss = get(ns, seed + 99i64);
        return tail(hit) + tail(miss);    // MOVES both
    }
    // main loops build() 2000x
    -> SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: 64000 byte(s) leaked in 2000 allocation(s)

Swapping the consumer for a READING one (`hit?.f3`) is clean, which is what localises it to the move.

THE IR IS EXPLICIT about both halves. At the call site the caller emits
    %hit25 = load { i64, i64, i64, i64 }, ptr %hit23
    store { i64, i64, i64, i64 } zeroinitializer, ptr %hit23   ; move-out sentinel
    %call = call i64 @tail({ i64, i64, i64, i64 } %hit25)
and its scope-exit `BoxedEnumDrop` then reloads that slot, reads tag 0, and skips. `@tail` contains no `free` and no drop call at all — it recovers the payload with `inttoptr` (`%enumbox.mv`) and returns. So the sentinel hands ownership to a callee that never takes it.

WHY IT WAS NEVER SEEN SEQUENTIALLY, measured rather than assumed. The same source with `KARAC_AUTO_PAR=0` reports clean at 2000 iterations — but not because anything frees. `objdump` on the sequential binary shows NO user function calling `malloc`, while the auto-par binary has one in `__par_branch_0_0`; the ASAN size-class stats agree (2176 allocations in the 48-byte class under auto-par, none sequentially). Everything inlines into one function, the box never escapes, and LLVM promotes the allocation away entirely. Under auto-par the pointer crosses a thread boundary through the return struct, so it is a real allocation and the missing free becomes a real leak.

SO THE FIX IS ONE OF: make a by-value enum parameter adopt its boxed payload (the callee frees, which is what the sentinel already assumes), or suppress the caller's sentinel for a boxed-payload binding so the caller keeps ownership. The first matches the ownership model the move-out sentinel is written against; the second is smaller but makes `move` not mean move for this one shape. Deciding between them is the work.

SEVERITY MEDIUM, not high: it is a bounded leak on a shape that is correct in value, whereas its parent row was silent data corruption. Reachable today only through auto-par, where the allocation survives the optimizer.

