WHICH ENUM EACH SIDE PICKS. The interpreter takes the LAST DECLARED: `register_items` writes `env["A"]` once per enum and the later write wins. The typechecker does NOT follow declaration order -- with `First` first it picks `First`, and with the order REVERSED (`Second` declared first) it still picks `First`; renaming to `Zebra` (first) and `Alpha` (second) makes it pick `Alpha`. All three data points are consistent with the alphabetically-first enum name, i.e. a sorted or otherwise name-ordered map rather than a deliberate rule. Deterministic across repeated runs (checked 5x), so this is not hash-order nondeterminism -- but it is not a rule anyone chose either.

WHY IT IS FILED RATHER THAN FIXED. There are three defensible answers and the spec picks none of them: (a) reject a bare ambiguous variant at resolve with a diagnostic naming both enums and suggesting the qualified form, (b) resolve it type-directed from the expected type, so `want_second(A)` means `Second.A`, or (c) keep an arbitrary winner and merely make the backends agree. (c) is the cheapest and the worst -- it cements a name-ordering accident as language semantics. (a) is the smallest honest fix and matches the language's existing preference for explicitness; it is also a BREAKING change for any program relying on today's bare form with a collision. Making that call is the work here, and it belongs to whoever owns the surface, not to the session that found it.

NOTE: with NO collision, the bare form is fine and must keep working -- the pattern matcher classifies a bare PascalCase identifier as a unit-variant pattern only when `env.get(name)` returns a unit `EnumVariant`, and breaking that reopens B-2026-06-30-14. Any fix has to leave the unambiguous case untouched; tests/interpreter.rs::bare_unit_variant_still_resolves_when_unambiguous pins it.

