THE FLAT CASE IS CORRECT, which is what makes this specifically a DEPTH bug rather than a broken `clone`: `let flat: Vec[i64] = [1, 1]; let mut f = flat.clone(); f[0] = 77;` prints `orig[0]=1 copy[0]=77` under the interpreter, exactly right. Only the elements that are themselves heap collections are aliased instead of copied.

WHY HIGH. `x.clone()` before mutating is THE defensive idiom, and the failure is a SILENT WRONG ANSWER rather than a crash -- the program runs to completion and prints something plausible. It also lands on the interpreter specifically, which is not a niche surface: `karac run --interp` is the documented fallback, and CLAUDE.md routes regex, Arrow-IPC and `gpu.dispatch` programs to the interpreter automatically under `karac run`, so a program using any of those gets the shallow-clone semantics without ever asking for them.

HOW IT SURFACED, and why it hid. Kata #289's differential asserts Conway invariants -- a blinker must DIFFER after one step and RETURN after two, a block must never change. Under the interpreter the differential reported `invariant failures 1` where all three compiled surfaces reported 0. The one that failed is the only invariant asserting a DIFFERENCE: `step(osc)` mutated `osc`, the aliasing carried the mutation into `blinker`, and the two then compared equal. Every other invariant asserts SAMENESS and so passed VACUOUSLY -- aliasing makes `a == b` trivially true. An oracle suite built only from stability properties would have reported green on this bug.

NOT B-2026-06-18-9, which was the CODEGEN side mis-building a shallow copy for a `ref`/`mut ref` BORROWED receiver. This is the INTERPRETER, on an OWNED receiver, and codegen is the leg that gets it right.

    fn main() {
        let deep: Vec[Vec[Vec[i64]]] = [[[5]]];
        let mut d = deep.clone();
        d[0][0][0] = 42i64;                     // codegen failed: Index assignment target
        println(f"{d[0][0][0]}");              // must be a variable
    }

Two levels compile and run: `d[0][0] = 99i64` on a `Vec[Vec[i64]]` is fine, both as a local and through a `mut ref` parameter (kata #289's `board[r][c] = board[r][c] | 2i64` is the natural instance and builds cleanly). Adding one more level of nesting is what trips it, so the store path evidently peels exactly one index off the assignment target and requires what remains to be a bare identifier.

The interpreter accepts all three depths, so this is also a run/build split -- `--interp` runs the program and codegen refuses it -- though the honest framing is a missing codegen case rather than a wrong answer: the compiler REFUSES rather than miscompiling, and the message is actionable enough to point at the construct.

SIBLINGS in the same family, both fixed, both one-off shapes of the same peel: B-2026-08-09-21 (a nested index whose base is a STRUCT FIELD, `h.data[i][j]`) and B-2026-08-10-5 (index-assign through a TUPLE FIELD of Slice type, `p.0[0] = x`). A general place-expression store path would subsume this row and both of those, which is probably the fix worth making rather than adding a third special case.

Severity medium, not high: three-level nesting is uncommon in the corpus (kata #289 hit it only in a reduced probe, not in a solver), and a refusal at build time is far less dangerous than the silent wrong answer in B-2026-08-20-32, which the same probe found.

REPRO:
    fn main() {
        let mut m: Map[i64, Vec[i64]] = Map.new();
        m.insert(1i64, [7i64]);
        println(f"{m[1i64][0]}");        // codegen: nested indexed read on 'm'
    }
The interpreter prints 7. `karac build` and `karac run` (JIT) both refuse.

WHY IT IS A DIFFERENT FIX FROM B-2026-08-20-33, not an oversight in it. That row generalised the CHAIN -- `container_place_name` recurses through index links and resolves each to a synth identifier -- but every element-pointer helper it calls (`lower_indexed_elem_ptr_vec` / `_slice` / `_array`) computes a GEP into contiguous storage. A Map element has no such pointer: the runtime hash map owns its slots and a lookup returns a VALUE, not a stable interior address. So the resolver correctly returns `Ok(None)` for a Map base and the caller falls through to its original diagnostic. Widening it means either a Map-specific `lookup -> temporary -> index the temporary` lowering (which is what the workaround `let row = m[k]; row[i]` already spells by hand and which is only valid for a READ), or an interior-pointer accessor on the runtime map. The WRITE half (`m[k][i] = v`) has the same wall and is the harder one, since a temporary would have to be written back.

SEVERITY LOW: the workaround is one line, natural to write, and does not distort the program -- binding the row to a local is how most people spell this anyway. It is filed because it is a run/build split the compiler REFUSES rather than miscompiles, and because it is now the only nested-read shape left refused.

