FIVE SHAPES MEASURED, and the boundary is narrower than "any place, any payload":

  place                          callee does          result
  identifier (`mut n`)           s = s + "!"          clean
  plain struct field             s = s + "!"          LEAKS 8 bytes/call
  NESTED field (`b.inner.name`)  s = s + "!"          clean
  `shared struct` field          s = s + "!"          LEAKS 3 bytes/call
  plain field, `Vec[String]`     v = ["x", "y"]       clean

So it is a PLAIN (one-hop) struct field holding a `String`, whose callee REASSIGNS the `mut ref` parameter. The callee holds a pointer to the caller's field slot; `s = s + "!"` allocates a fresh buffer and overwrites the slot with it, and nothing frees what the slot held before the store.

THE THREE CLEAN COLUMNS ARE THE INTERESTING PART and none of them is explained here -- they are recorded as measured so whoever takes this starts from the difference rather than from the leak. A nested field reaches the same `mut_ref_place_arg_ptr` arm through an extra `mut_ref_place_root_ptr` hop and does NOT leak; a `Vec` payload through the identical arm does NOT leak, so the missing release is String-specific rather than generic to heap payloads. The `shared struct` column leaks a DIFFERENT amount (3 bytes/call, the size of the post-append `n!` rather than the pre-append `n`), which suggests the `shared_mut_ref_place_arg_ptr` arm loses a different allocation than the plain-field arm does -- possibly two distinct defects sharing one entry point.

BOTH CALL FORMS ARE AFFECTED as of B-2026-08-21-38: the method spelling (`h.app(mut b.name)`) leaks the identical 392 bytes now that it takes the same place pointer. Before that commit the method spelling did not leak here -- it DOUBLE-FREED instead, which is why the current state is the strictly better of the two and not a regression.

The likely direction is a release of the slot's existing body immediately before the store that lands a reassigned `mut ref` parameter -- i.e. treating assignment through a `mut ref` param the way `StmtKind::Assign` to a heap-bearing place is already treated -- but the three clean columns say the real gap is narrower than that, and this row records only what was measured.

