NOT A SURPRISE, and that is the point of filing it: the slice-3 entry in docs/implementation_checklist/phase-5-diagnostics.md says in as many words "No codegen support -- programs that USE the existential at call sites work end-to-end through the typechecker only; codegen lowering for existentials is the slice 4+ continuation", and the parent `impl Trait` epic is marked shipped. So a documented deferral is sitting behind a checked-off feature, with the failure mode being the run-vs-build divergence the kata rules call non-negotiable. It needs a row in the queue, not a sentence in a checklist.

The fix is a dispatcher arm in `compile_method_call` for an existential receiver. It should be tractable: the single-witness rule (B-2026-07-08-1) already guarantees exactly one concrete type per monomorphization, so the receiver's real type is statically known -- the missing piece is carrying the witness from the typechecker to codegen, not inventing dynamic dispatch.

SPLIT OUT of B-2026-08-22-12 during its witness-matrix probe. NOT a run-vs-build divergence -- it is check-RED, so both backends agree. It is a false positive on valid code, and it blocks the most ordinary builder shape there is.

  trait Counter { fn bumped(self) -> Self; fn value(ref self) -> i64; }
  struct Ctr { n: i64 }
  impl Counter for Ctr {
      fn bumped(self) -> Ctr { Ctr { n: self.n + 1 } }
      fn value(ref self) -> i64 { self.n }
  }
  fn make(n: i64) -> impl Counter { Ctr { n: n } }
  fn main() { let c = make(1); let d = c.bumped(); println(d.value()); }

  -> error[typecheck]: cannot infer type parameter 'Counter'; add a type annotation to this binding
  -> error[typecheck]: no method 'value' on type parameter 'Counter'; add a trait bound
                       declaring it (e.g. `Counter: SomeTrait`)

`dispatch_existential_receiver_method` substitutes `Self -> Type::TypeParam(<trait name>)`, the same move the type-param receiver path makes. On a type PARAMETER that is right, because the param carries its bounds. On an existential it is not: the result is a bare `TypeParam("Counter")` with no bounds attached, so the value that comes back has lost the one thing the existential guaranteed about it -- that it implements the trait. Hence the second diagnostic, which advises adding a bound to something the user never wrote and cannot name.

The correct answer is that `Self` on an existential receiver IS that existential: `c.bumped()` should type as the same `impl Counter`, same origin, so it stays opaque and keeps the trait surface. Note the diagnostics point at the CALLER's own correct code and name an internal spelling ("type parameter 'Counter'") that appears nowhere in the source -- the same shape as the projection failure B-2026-08-22-4 fixed, where an unresolved `Src.Item` surfaced as "cannot infer type parameter 'Src'".

Once typed correctly it should also RUN: B-2026-08-22-12's substitution already gives codegen a concrete witness for the existential, and `Ctr.bumped` is an ordinary impl method, so nothing further is needed in the backend. Verified as a lower bound: the same program with `-> Ctr` spelled concretely builds and runs on all four surfaces.

