NOT A REGRESSION from any recent row -- the arm has matched `..` since it was written. It went unnoticed because no test formatted an `Fn`-typed annotation; the sibling `#[no_effect]` test pins precisely this property for attributes and there was no counterpart for types.

design.md's Vec table says `insert` "Inserts at index; panics if out of bounds" and `remove` "Removes at index, shifts remaining; panics if out of bounds". THE INTERPRETER DOES EXACTLY THAT. NEITHER COMPILED BACKEND CHECKS AT ALL -- `karac run` (JIT) and `karac build` (AOT) behave identically to each other and differently from `--interp`, so this is a run-vs-build divergence AND a memory-safety hole in a safe-language operation.

MEASURED, on a `Vec[i64]` holding 2 elements:

    op                  --interp                     karac run / karac build
    v.insert(7, 9)      panic, exit 101              glibc `free(): invalid pointer`, exit 134
    v.remove(7)         panic, exit 101              glibc `free(): invalid pointer`, exit 134
    v.swap_remove(7)    panic, exit 101              NO ERROR, exit 0 -- see below
    v[7]                panic, exit 101              panic `vec index out of bounds`, exit 101

The last row is the control and it is why this is a bug rather than a policy: plain INDEXING is correctly checked in codegen, so the checked-vs-unchecked split is inconsistent within one type, not a deliberate uniform 'no bounds checks in release'.

SWAP_REMOVE IS THE WORST OF THE THREE because it does not crash. On the AOT build `v.swap_remove(7)` returns `0`, sets `len` to 1, and drops the element that was at index 1 -- a SILENT WRONG ANSWER with a clean exit 0. Under the JIT the same call returned `94405721258608`, i.e. a live heap POINTER VALUE handed back as an `i64`, so the read is genuinely out of bounds and leaks memory contents rather than reading a benign zero.

ROOT CAUSE, in `src/codegen/vec_method.rs`. The `insert` arm loads `len`, computes `move_count = len - idx`, and hands that to `build_memmove` as an element count. For `idx > len` the subtraction is NEGATIVE and reaches memmove as a huge unsigned byte count, which is the heap corruption. No bounds comparison is emitted anywhere in the arm. The omission is deliberate and documented in tree: the `swap_remove` arm's comment reads "Mirrors `remove`'s no-codegen-bounds-check precedent (the interpreter checks + panics)" -- so the three methods share one 'precedent' whose premise (that the interpreter's check covers the compiled backends) does not hold, because the compiled backends never run the interpreter.

REPRODUCE:

    fn main() {
        let mut v: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
        v.push(11); v.push(22);
        let x = v.swap_remove(7);
        println(f"returned {x} len {v.len()}");
    }

`karac run --interp` panics; `karac build` prints `returned 0 len 1` and exits 0.

SEVERITY high: silent memory corruption and an out-of-bounds read whose value is observable to the program, from a spec'd-as-panicking operation, on the DEFAULT execution path. `insert(len, v)` (append at the end) is legal and works correctly on both backends -- the boundary is exactly `idx > len` for insert and `idx >= len` for remove/swap_remove; negative indices corrupt too (`munmap_chunk(): invalid pointer`).

