REPRO (rejected; no stdlib involvement):
  struct W[T] { v: Vec[T] }
  impl[T: Ord] W[T] {
      fn helper(mut ref self, i: i64) { let _ = i; }
      fn make(x: Vec[T]) -> W[T] {
          let mut w = W { v: x };
          w.helper(0);   // error[typecheck]: method 'helper' is not callable on
          w              // this `W`: trait bound `T: Ord` is not satisfied;
      }                  // `T` does not implement `Ord`
  }
The bound reported unsatisfied is the impl's OWN bound, which `T` carries by construction inside that impl -- the diagnostic asks for something it already has.

NARROWED to a conjunction; either condition alone is fine:
  (a) ASSOCIATED fn (no `self` receiver) + BOUNDED impl   -> REJECTED
  (b) INSTANCE method, local receiver     + BOUNDED impl   -> accepted
  (c) ASSOCIATED fn (no `self` receiver)  + UNBOUNDED impl -> accepted
Needing both no-receiver AND a bound is why it survived B-2026-07-03-20 (same family: a bound on a generic impl's own param breaking method resolution), whose fix was exercised through instance methods.

ROOT CAUSE: `check_impl_block` (src/typechecker/items.rs) called `check_function(method, Some(&self_type), &[])` -- an EMPTY enclosing generic scope -- for every method. `check_function` builds its scope as `enclosing_generics` plus the fn's own params, so the impl's `T` was absent from every method body. An instance method masked it: its `self_type` is already `W[TypeParam(T)]`, and `first_unsatisfied_bound` (src/typechecker/env.rs) explicitly SKIPS a `Type::TypeParam` argument as undischargeable-yet. With no receiver nothing supplies that binding, so `T` in the signature lowered as `Named { name: "T", args: [] }`, the local `W { v: x }` inherited it, and the bound check discharged a NAMED type against the impl table -- where no `impl Ord for T` exists. The identical `Named`-instead-of-`TypeParam` fallthrough is already documented one arm below in the GAT `AssocType` branch, which fixes it the same way.

FIX: pass the impl's generic param names (`&gp`, already computed in that function for `validate_all_bounds`) instead of `&[]`. One argument.

BLAST RADIUS is wider than the repro suggests, and is the reason to keep this row: it applied to EVERY associated function of every bounded generic impl, user code included -- and the constructor-plus-helper shape (a `new`/`from` that calls a private method on the value it is building) is the most common associated-fn shape there is. Nothing in the corpus hit it because no bounded generic impl in kara/, kara-katas/ or the stdlib had an associated fn calling a method on a local of its own type; `examples/heap.kara` came closest, and its `Heap.new()` returns a literal without calling anything.

TESTS: tests/typechecker.rs -- test_assoc_fn_sees_impl_generic_bound_on_local_receiver (the repro), test_instance_method_local_receiver_under_impl_bound_still_ok (the leg that always worked), test_assoc_fn_unsatisfied_bound_still_rejected_at_call_site (a genuinely unsatisfied CONCRETE instantiation is still rejected -- the fix must not blanket-disable the gate).

