CORRECTION TO THIS ROW'S OWN "READING" (recorded because the hypothesis was confidently stated and wrong, and it would have sent the next reader to the wrong subsystem). The row guessed a move/ownership accounting problem -- "the owned receiver's heap-carrying elements are dropped (or shallow-copied and then dropped) while the Vec's length survives" -- and advised checking drop/move accounting under ASAN for a use-after-free. It is not a drop bug and there is no UAF: nothing is freed. It is monomorph SELECTION. A probe printing each element immediately after `h.pop_one()` returned, inside the loop and long before any scope exit, already showed the element empty at the very first pop -- which rules out an end-of-function drop by construction. `o[i].len()` reads 0, a clean zero rather than the garbage a freed control block would give.

The two narrowing conditions the row could not explain both fall out of the real cause: the element type matters because the default prototype's base layout is all-`i64` (so a 24-byte String control block is read as one i64), and `T = i64` is "correct" only by that coincidence -- its symbol table shows the same unmangled `Heap.pop_one` the broken case uses.

The row's third condition -- "generic impl" -- is right, and its observation that rebinding a local struct LITERAL is correct while rebinding `self` is not was the decisive clue: it isolates `ExprKind::SelfValue` as the discriminator, since an Identifier RHS resolves through the span table and `self` cannot.

WHAT THE ROW GOT RIGHT AND EARNED: the instruction not to assume B-2026-08-25-5's fix covered this. It did not -- these are two different sites separated by ~5000 lines, and the shared cause is only that `SelfValue` is not `Identifier("self")`. `SelfValue`-vs-`Identifier` is a long-running family rather than a one-off: 29 rows in this ledger already reference `SelfValue`, and `src/codegen/` carries ~115 explicit `ExprKind::SelfValue` arms across 25 files, each one a site where someone had to remember that `self` is not an identifier. Two are worth naming here because they bear directly on this row. `enum_inst_type_of_expr` (expr_ops.rs) consults the name-keyed table for `Identifier` ONLY, and method_call.rs:8506 carries an explicit in-line workaround for exactly that omission, with a comment saying so -- so the same gap has already been patched once at a call site instead of at its source. And B-2026-07-17-3 fixed the sibling half of THIS statement handler (the move-suppression cap-zeroing for `let mut b = self;`) without the instantiation record next to it being given the same treatment.

The general repair -- teaching `enum_inst_type_of_expr` about `SelfValue` and dropping the workaround -- was deliberately NOT taken here. It would not have fixed this bug (the `let` site never calls that function; it goes through the span table), so it is scope beyond the row and would need its own suite run to justify. Worth a dedicated sweep, filed as its own item rather than smuggled into a bug fix.

