MEASURED ON PRISTINE main (fda65f4), with every local change stashed -- this is not a regression from any in-flight work.

THE CONTRAST THAT NAMES THE BUG. The DIRECT cycle builds fine:
  struct Node { kids: Vec[Node], name: String }   -> builds, prints 0
That is B-2026-07-28-3's repro, and its guard is why it works. That row installed the cycle guard in `field_copy_supported`'s Vec arm as a STACK-MEMBERSHIP test: "is the element type already on the walk stack". For `Node` the element IS the struct being walked, so it trips. For a cycle of length two or more it does not -- walking `Outer` sees element `Inner`, which is not on the stack, so the arm returns `true` (copy-supported) AND STOPS THE WALK. `Inner.owner: Outer`, the edge that closes the cycle, is never examined.

The emitter has no such stopping rule: `deep_copy_one_aggregate_field` descends a `Vec[struct]`'s elements via `deep_copy_vec_aggregate_elements_in_place`, which inlines a per-element `deep_copy_struct_heap_fields_in_place`, which descends that struct's own fields. So it took the `Inner.owner` edge straight back to `Outer` and recursed until the 16 MiB compiler stack was gone. The copy is UNROLLED at emission, so its depth would have to equal the runtime data depth: no finite emission exists, which is why declining is the only correct answer.

FIX: ask about REACHABILITY rather than membership, so the analysis walks exactly as deep as the emitter -- which is what B-2026-07-28-3's own comment says it intended ("Consulting `stack` here makes the analysis walk exactly as deep as the emitter"). The new `vec_elem_copy_emission_terminates` declines when the element is an ancestor (the original case), can REACH an ancestor (the mutual cycle), or reaches itself (a cycle wholly below the current walk).

DELIBERATELY NARROW: an ACYCLIC element type still answers "terminates" and keeps the arm's unconditional `true`, so programs that compile today emit byte-identical IR. Only a genuine cycle newly declines, and declining means the param falls back to caller-retains -- the same conservative treatment every other non-copyable field shape already gets.

KNOWN REMAINDER, not fixed here and not new: a declined self-referential struct is ALSO excluded from own-by-transfer (B-2026-08-05-33 excludes it deliberately: the callee may store the alias into an owning container, so a param drop could free what the container now owns). So a by-value param of a cyclic struct is caller-retains, and a callee that MOVES a field out of it can double-free -- which is what `std.cli`'s `Parser.parse(self)` does. That is why B-2026-08-25-2 stays open rather than riding in on this fix: terminating the emission is necessary for it and not sufficient.

