CORRECTION TO THIS ROW'S OWN NARROWING -- it was wrong on TWO of its three stated conditions, and both errors were mine when I filed it.

(2) "rebinding `self`" is NOT required. `fn into_vec(self) -> Vec[T] { self.xs }` -- moving the field straight out of the owned receiver, no `let mut h = self`, no sibling call -- double-frees identically. The rebinding appeared necessary only because the filed repro happened to contain one. The real requirement is that heap-owning content be transferred OUT of the owned receiver; a method that only reads (`fn take(self) -> i64 { self.xs.len() }`) is clean. This matters because it widens the blast radius considerably: `into_vec` / `into_inner` / `into_sorted` on any generic container is the affected shape, not just the rebinding idiom.

(3) "`T = String` is correct" is FALSE, and the way it was measured is worth recording because the same trap will catch the next reader. String LITERALS live in static rodata with cap 0, so the drop's `cap > 0` guard skips them and the second free is a silent no-op -- the fixture passes while the bug is fully present. Rebuilt with elements allocated on the heap (`xs.push(f"item{i}")`), `T = String` double-frees exactly like `T = Vec[i64]`. A literal is not a specimen of its type for memory-ownership purposes.

What the row got right: "generic impl" is required (the non-generic twin deep-copies elements correctly), a scalar `T` is genuinely correct (no inner buffer to alias), and the instruction to lead with ASAN -- which named both free sites and the allocation on the first run, after prose reasoning had already produced one wrong hypothesis on the parent row.

REMAINDER, NOT FIXED HERE: making the entry-copy element-deep exposes a pre-existing LEAK on the drop side, filed as B-2026-08-25-11. In a monomorph whose local receiver binding is recorded head-only, the struct drop mangles `__karac_drop_struct_Heap$Vec` and its per-element drop is `karac_drop_Vec` -- an EMPTY STUB (`ret void`), because the element type was erased to a bare `Vec`. Before this fix the callee's elements aliased the caller's, so the caller's drop freed them and the stub's no-op-ness was invisible; that aliasing WAS the double free. Now the callee owns real copies that its own drop does not free. Measured honestly: the `pop`-shaped repro leaks 24 bytes in 2 allocations post-fix (it aborted before completing pre-fix), and a TEMPORARY-receiver shape goes from 16 bytes in 2 allocations to 40 in 5 -- the 3 added allocations are exactly the element copies this fix introduces. Trading unbounded memory corruption for a bounded leak is a strict improvement, but it is not the end of the story and is tracked rather than glossed.

