CORRECTION TO THIS ROW'S OWN PREMISE AND ITS WARNING -- both were wrong, and both were mine.

The title said the temporary "is never dropped by the caller". It IS dropped: the emitted IR contains `call void @__karac_drop_struct_Heap(ptr %__urecv_tmp)`. What is wrong is WHICH drop -- the unmangled outer-only one. That is not a pedantic distinction, it points at a different fix: this needed an existing drop corrected, not a missing drop added.

The row then warned that only TWO of three element buffers leaked, so "something already frees the third", and cautioned that registering a drop for all three risked converting a leak into a double free. That was an artifact of the fixture: every element was 8 bytes, so the leak report could not distinguish them. Re-run with sizes 8/16/24, ALL THREE leak. There is no mystery third free and no double-free risk, and the caution sent the next reader chasing something that does not exist.

The evidence that should have caught this at filing time was already in the report: the temporary's 72-byte OUTER buffer never appeared among the leaks. A value that is "never dropped" leaks its outer buffer too. Its absence meant a drop had run -- which is exactly the outer-only drop this fix replaces. The lesson is narrow and reusable: in a leak report, what is MISSING from the list is evidence, and a uniform-size fixture cannot tell you which of N identical allocations survived. Give elements distinct sizes whenever the question is *which*.

