THIRD SHAPE OF ONE FAMILY. The `let` site in `src/codegen/stmts.rs` records a binding's concrete generic instantiation so a later sibling call can bind the impl's type params from it. Its chain was: a concrete generic ANNOTATION, then a `SelfValue` name-keyed lookup (B-2026-08-25-7), then the span-keyed `enum_inst_type_from_span`, then `field_move_out_struct_inst` (B-2026-07-15-24). A struct LITERAL matches none of them -- and unlike the `self` case it is not that the span table holds the wrong (generic) form, it holds NO ENTRY AT ALL for a literal. So `b` got no instantiation, `compile_method_call`'s generic-impl arm produced an empty subst, and `mangle_mono_name` maps an empty subst back to the BASE NAME -- the call went to the unmangled prototype built at the all-`i64` base layout.

PROOF, from `nm` on an -O0 AOT build of the repro. PRE-FIX the binary contains `Bag.build$i64`, `Bag.build$struct` and `Bag.take$i64`, `Bag.take$struct` -- but a bare `Bag.arrange` and a bare `Bag.swap2` with NO `$i64` / `$struct` siblings, i.e. one prototype serving both instantiations. POST-FIX all four exist: `arrange$i64`, `arrange$struct`, `swap2$i64`, `swap2$struct`. Reading a 24-byte String control block as one i64 is the segfault; `T = i64` is correct only because the base layout happens to match, the same coincidence B-2026-08-25-7 documented.

FIX: at the `let` site, reconstruct the literal's type arguments from the struct's DECLARED generic parameter names and resolve them through the active monomorph's substitution via the existing `concrete_generic_struct_inst`. That helper returns None when nothing was bound -- no monomorph in flight, or an already-concrete literal -- so the arm is inert outside the case it exists for. Ordered before the span fallback, since for a literal the span table has nothing to offer.

FOUND BY registering `PriorityQueue[T: Ord]` (phase-11): `PriorityQueue.from(v)` is exactly this shape, so `from(...)` followed by ANY `pop()` segfaulted at `T = String` while `new()` + `push` + `pop` was fine. The narrowing that isolated it is worth keeping: `from` + `len` / `push` / `clear` all PASS, and only `pop` crashes -- because `heapify`'s swap corrupts slots that only `pop` later reads back. A test that checked `len` after `from` would have reported green.

