ROOT CAUSE, one for all six. The span record is PRE-monomorphization, so for an RHS inside a generic impl it holds the GENERIC form (`Bag[T]`). That is WORSE than holding nothing: it satisfies the chain and stops the search, and the sibling call site cannot bind concrete params from `T -> T`, so `mangle_mono_name` maps the empty subst back to the base name. FIX: resolve the record's bare params through the active monomorph's substitution via the existing `concrete_generic_struct_inst` -- the same helper the struct-literal arm uses. One arm, six forms, no new special case.

NO USER ESCAPE HATCH, which is what makes this high rather than medium. An explicit `Bag[T]` annotation does NOT rescue these: the annotation arm requires a CONCRETE generic type expression and `Bag[T]` is not one (measured -- it build-fails). The only workaround was laundering the value through a struct literal, i.e. the single form that already had an arm.

THE SCALAR LEG IS LOAD-BEARING. At `T = i64` all six were SILENTLY CORRECT, because the base prototype's layout happens to match -- the same coincidence B-2026-08-25-7 and -25 documented. A scalar-only test reports green on every one of them, which is why the sweep test asserts both instantiations.

