                                                          iteration)

So nothing miscompiles and no backend disagrees; what is missing is the
compile error the spec promises. That is why this is `low` and separate: the
cost of leaving it is that a program the spec calls illegal is accepted, not
that any program computes the wrong answer.

WHERE IT WOULD GO. `typechecker/gpu_safe.rs`'s `check_gpu_closure_captures`
is a shadow-aware free-variable walk over a closure body against
`flatten_local_scope_snapshot()`, called from the single closure-typing
chokepoint in `closures.rs`. A `LazyLock.new` argument reaching that
chokepoint with a non-empty capture set against enclosing LOCALS is exactly
the rejection this rule wants; the walk is already written and already
proven on the GPU rule. The work is recognizing the `LazyLock.new` argument
position at that chokepoint (the ctor is recognized in `items.rs`, but the
ordering against closure typing needs checking) and giving the diagnostic a
code.

NOTE ON SCOPE INTERACTION: at MODULE scope there are no locals to capture,
so this rule only ever bites the local `let c: LazyLock[T] = ...` form --
which design.md's family table does not mention and B-2026-08-26-3 shipped
anyway, to avoid a run-vs-build divergence. If the local form is later
declared illegal instead, this row closes with it.

