`10:1 2:20` is exactly what an 8-BYTE swap over the buffer produces: as i64s the store is [1, 10, 2, 20], swapping elements 0 and 1 at scalar width gives [10, 1, 2, 20], which reads back as `(10, 1), (2, 20)`. So `T` was sized as one i64 rather than as the 16-byte tuple.

DIAGNOSED, not guessed. Instrumenting `compile_generic_call` to print each monomorph's substitution frames:

    T = (i64, i64)   [mono] Bag.outer$struct  subst=["T"] names={} tes=[]
                     [mono] Bag.sw            subst=[]    names={} tes=[]     <- T LOST
    T = i64          [mono] Bag.outer$i64     subst=["T"] names={"T":"i64"}
                     [mono] Bag.sw$i64        subst=["T"] names={"T":"i64"}
    T = String       [mono] Bag.outer$struct$T_ct_String  names={"T":"String"}
                     [mono] Bag.sw$struct$T_ct_String     names={"T":"String"}

The nested call is reached through `span_tables.call_type_subs`, a frame of (param_name, concrete_NAME) pairs the typechecker records in `record_call_type_subs` via `type_to_concrete_or_param_name`. A tuple has no name, so nothing is recorded, the enclosing monomorph's binding cannot be flattened through, and the callee is emitted with an EMPTY substitution -- `Bag.sw`, not `Bag.sw$struct`, a different symbol from the one the working single-level call emits.

REPRODUCE B -- the mangling half of the same cause, loud instead of silent. `mono_mangle_token_for_type_expr` and `type_expr_mangle_seg` both map every non-`Path` shape to one opaque token, so two tuple instantiations of the same generic share a symbol:

    struct W[=T] { v: Vec[T] }
    impl[T] W[T] { fn add(mut ref self, x: T) { self.v.push(x); } }
    fn main() {
        let mut w: W[(i64, i64)] = W { v: Vec.new() };   w.add((1, 2));
        let mut s: W[(String, i64)] = W { v: Vec.new() }; s.add(("a", 1));
