    error: codegen failed: Module verification failed: "Call parameter type does not
    match function signature! ... call void @\"W.add$struct\"(ptr %s, { { ptr, i64, i64 }, i64 } ...)"

SINGLE LEVEL IS FINE, which is what makes this hard to see: `main` calling `b.sw(0, 1)` directly emits `Bag.sw$struct` and swaps correctly. It takes one method calling another on `self`.

CONSEQUENCE: `PriorityQueue[(i64, i64)]` compiles and prints garbage. Every `PriorityQueue` method lives on `impl[T: Ord]` and `push` reaches `sift_up` reaches `outranks`/`swap`, so it is nested by construction. Pushing (3,30), (1,10), (2,20) and peeking after each gives `3:30 / 1:10 / 1:10` under the interpreter and `3:30 / 3:30 / 1:30` on AOT -- the last a tuple assembled from two different elements. `PriorityQueue[i64]`, `PriorityQueue[String]` and `PriorityQueue[<derived-Ord struct>]` are all correct, so it is not the stdlib path and not the element SIZE (the 16-byte struct works); it is specifically that the type argument has no name.

FIX SHAPE: the substitution channel needs a `TypeExpr` leg beside the name leg, all the way through -- `record_call_type_subs` (which today drops what `type_to_concrete_or_param_name` cannot name), `call_type_subs` -> `subst_type_exprs`, and the two mangle helpers. `type_subst_type_exprs` already exists and is exactly this leg for Vec/VecDeque elements (B-2026-07-13-2/-3); a tuple needs the same treatment. Not attempted here: it touches every generic call, and the row it was found under is a typecheck false-positive.

SOURCE NOTE: found verifying B-2026-08-27-33. Reproduces on ae55c85 with the bound REMOVED, so it is not caused by that fix -- but that fix does newly make `PriorityQueue[(i64, i64)]` reach it.

DIAGNOSIS CORRECTION (at fix time). The row states the binding is lost in `record_call_type_subs` because `type_to_concrete_or_param_name` cannot name a tuple, and prescribes a `TypeExpr` leg through `call_type_subs`. That is wrong, and instrumenting the table rather than the mangle is what showed it: `span_tables.call_type_subs` is `None` at BOTH the outer `b.outer()` span and the inner `self.sw(..)` span, and it is `None` in the WORKING `T = i64` case too. A channel that is empty in both arms of the comparison cannot be the difference. The original instrumentation printed only `subst`/`names`/`tes` and inferred the cause from the empty `names`; printing the FRAME itself, and re-running the same probe at `T = i64` as a control, is what corrected it.

The real binder is `method_call.rs`'s impl-method dispatch, which derives EXPLICIT generic args from the receiver's recorded instantiation and hands them to `compile_generic_call`. Its explicit-args loop binds the LLVM type for ANY `TypeExpr` but the name only for a `TypeKind::Path`. So the tuple's LLVM type was always correct at the outer call -- which is why `Bag.outer` mangled and ran fine -- and only the NAME was missing, which is what the next call needed. `record_call_type_subs` and the typechecker are untouched by the fix.

The free-function shape (`pick[T](a: T, b: T)` at two tuple instantiations) was not in the row at all; it reproduces the same `$struct` collision through `infer_type_args` and is fixed here too.

