That is an 8-byte swap over a 16-byte element: `T` fell to the `i64` unknown-name default.

NOT TUPLE-SPECIFIC, and that is what distinguishes it from B-2026-08-27-40, whose whole subject is "the substitution channel is keyed by NAME and a tuple has none". The identical program at `Bag[String]` SEGFAULTS (exit 139) on `karac build` while the interpreter prints `bb aa`; at `Bag[i64]` both backends are correct. A named element is no protection, so the cause cannot be namelessness.

THE RETURNING VARIANT IS THE LOUD HALF of the same defect: `fn first[T](v: ref Vec[T]) -> T` called as `first(self.xs)` from a generic-impl method fails module verification -- `Function return type does not match operand type of return inst! ret i64 %call / { i64, i64 }` -- and fails at `Bag[String]` too. Where the callee returns nothing (`swap01`), the LLVM signature is a bare `ptr` and there is no mismatch for the verifier to catch, which is why that half is silent.

THE CONTROL THAT PINS IT is the same callee reached with an IDENTIFIER argument and no impl at all:

    fn swap01[T](v: mut ref Vec[T]) { v.swap(0, 1); }
    fn main() {
        let mut t: Vec[(i64, i64)] = Vec.new();
        t.push((1, 10)); t.push((2, 20));
        swap01(mut t);
        ...
    }

correct on AOT for BOTH a tuple and a String element. So the trigger is the ARGUMENT SHAPE -- a field access -- not the element type, not the impl, and not the generic nesting.

DIAGNOSIS: the resolvers that bind a container param's element type all key on the argument's BINDING NAME. `resolve_collection_param_substs` matches `ExprKind::Identifier(arg_name)` and looks the element up via `arg_collection_head_elem(arg_name)`; `augment_subst_from_arg_elem_types` reads the same name-keyed var side-tables (`vec_elem_types` / `var_elem_type_exprs`). A `self.xs` argument is an `ExprKind::FieldAccess`, so every one of them declines and nothing binds `T`.

FIX SHAPE: give those resolvers a field-access arm. The element is already recoverable -- `resolve_generic_field_te(container, struct_name, field_te)` resolves a generic field's type through the receiver's recorded instantiation (`enum_inst_var_types["self"]`), which is exactly the channel B-2026-08-27-40 made reliable for a tuple and which has served named elements all along. The arm needs to read the field's declared `TypeExpr` from `struct_types`, resolve it there, and bind the container's element from the result, filling the LLVM `subst` and both name/TypeExpr maps together -- they must move as a set, per the note on `fresh_container_arg_elem_llvm`.

