    struct R { id: i64, tag: String }
    impl Drop for R { fn drop(mut ref self) { println(f"dR{self.id}") } }
    enum E { A(R), B }
    impl Drop for E { fn drop(mut ref self) { println("dE") } }
    struct T { n: i64 }

    impl T { fn take(ref self, b: E) -> i64 {
        let mut out: R = R { id: 0, tag: f"t0" };
        match b { E.A(r) => { out = r; } E.B => { } }
        println("m"); return out.id;
    } }
    fn main() { let t: T = T { n: 1 };
                let v: i64 = t.take(E.A(R { id: 8, tag: f"t8" })); println(f"v{v}") }

      karac run --interp  -> dR0 m dR8 dE v8
      jit / build         -> dR0 m dE dR8 v8

ONE `dR8` and one `dE` on both sides -- the counts are right and were fixed by
B-2026-08-30-55 (before it, `--interp` printed `dR0 m dR8 v8`, missing `dE`
entirely). What remains is the SEQUENCE: the interpreter runs the payload's body
before the enum shell's, and both compiled backends run the shell's first.

THE ARGUMENT MUST BE A FRESH TEMP, which is what makes this the frame's own
problem rather than a caller-ordering one. The NAMED spelling of the identical
program agrees on all three surfaces (`dR0 m dE dR8 v8`) -- there the caller's
binding owns the value and fires it, and the frame retracts. With a fresh temp
the frame is the sole owner, so both bodies come from inside it, and the order
it drains them in is the order in question.

WHICH SIDE IS RIGHT is not settled here, and the row deliberately does not
assert one. The shell-then-payload order the compiled backends produce is the
one a reader would predict from the enum being destroyed as a whole, and it is
also what the NAMED spelling produces on every backend -- which is the stronger
argument, since two spellings of one program should not disagree. That points at
the interpreter as the side to move, but the frame's drain order is shared
machinery and the change should be measured against the whole `Drop`-order
corpus rather than this one shape.

Bodies only: each runs exactly once, and the compiled binary is valgrind-clean.
LOW because nothing leaks and nothing double-frees; it matters for a `Drop` whose
side effects are ordered against a sibling's -- a lock released before a log
line, say -- and for kara-katas' `run` == `build` rule, which a kata printing
from two `Drop` bodies would trip.

