    impl Drop for R { fn drop(mut ref self) { println(f"dR{self.id}") } }
    enum E { A(R), B }
    impl Drop for E { fn drop(mut ref self) { println("dE") } }
    fn mk(n: i64) -> R { return R { id: n }; }

    fn go(c: bool) -> i64 {
        let e = E.A(mk(5));
        let _ = <BRANCH>;
        println("mid");
        return 7;
    }

with `c` FALSE, so the arm that runs is the one naming `e`:

    <BRANCH> = if c { E.A(mk(8)) } else { e }
        interp   dE mid            jit/aot   dE dR5 mid
    <BRANCH> = match c { true => E.A(mk(8)), _ => e }
        interp   dE dR5 mid        jit/aot   dE mid

The two spellings mean the same thing and each backend answers them
differently -- in OPPOSITE directions, so neither backend is simply the correct
one and no single side can be aligned to the other. The bare-statement spelling
of the `if` behaves as its `let _ =` twin does.

The value dropped is `E.A(R{5})`, so `dE dR5` is the transcript a bound local of
that type produces on every backend when nothing takes it (measured: the same
program with the branch replaced by a statement that does not mention `e`
prints `dE dR5`). That makes `dE` alone the under-drop and `dR5` the body that
goes missing -- a leak of the payload, not a doubling.

